<!--
  fail2ban decoder

  Wazuh 4.14.7 ships no decoder and no rules for fail2ban at all. Every line
  below returns "No decoder matched" out of the box; verified, not assumed.

  Observed format (fail2ban 0.11.1, logtarget = /var/log/fail2ban.log):
    2026-08-31 21:20:11,500 fail2ban.actions        [2368444]: NOTICE  [zimbra-admin] Ban 203.0.113.45
    2026-08-31 21:24:32,814 fail2ban.actions        [2368444]: NOTICE  [zimbra-admin] Unban 203.0.113.45
    2026-08-31 21:03:00,269 fail2ban.filter         [2368444]: INFO    [zimbra-webmail] Found 203.0.113.45 . 2026-08-31 21:03:00
    2026-08-31 21:25:00,100 fail2ban.actions        [2368444]: WARNING [sshd] 203.0.113.45 already banned

  Pre-decoding eats more of this line than it looks like it should. Measured on
  4.14.7 by probing one pattern at a time: the string a decoder sees contains
  neither the date nor the word "fail2ban" nor the severity, so anchoring on any
  of them matches nothing. Only the tail, from the jail name onward, is visible.
  The prematch therefore describes the shape of that tail and carries no anchor.

  Because that shape alone could occur in another product log, the base rule
  additionally requires the event to come from a file named fail2ban. The decoder
  identifies the format; the rule identifies the source.

  There is one child decoder, not several. Measured: when the first child's regex
  fails, Wazuh does not go on to try the next one, so a set of siblings only ever
  works for whichever form happens to be listed first. All four verbs are
  therefore matched by a single expression.

  "[jail] address already banned" is deliberately not decoded. Its address comes
  before the verb, which no single <order> can express alongside the other forms,
  and it carries nothing the original ban alert did not already report. Such
  lines still match the base rule and stay silent.

  The address is extracted as srcip, the field Wazuh treats as the source of an
  event. That is what lets these rules correlate with the rest of the ruleset,
  and what an active response command would read.

  The verb is stored as f2b_action rather than action: action is one of Wazuh
  static field names, and a rule matching on it is refused at load time with
  "Field action is static". Renaming avoids the whole class of collision.
-->
<decoder name="fail2ban">
  <prematch type="pcre2">\[[\w.\- ]+\]\s+(?:Ban|Unban|Restore Ban|Found)\s+\S</prematch>
</decoder>

<decoder name="fail2ban-action">
  <parent>fail2ban</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">\[([^\]]+)\]\s+(Ban|Unban|Restore Ban|Found)\s+(\S+)</regex>
  <order>jail, f2b_action, srcip</order>
</decoder>
